FEP first-episode psychosis patient, NAA N-acetyl aspartate, Glx glutamate + glutamine, Cho choline, Cr creatine, CALB Cramer-Rao lower bounds, FWHM full width half maximum, SMR signal-to-noise ratio, CSF", "bbox": {"x": 150.77900529640885, "y": 1867.4499999911839, "width": 662.5138121546962, "height": 68.65625}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "*Institutional units.", "bbox": {"x": 155.34806593489452, "y": 1954.414583281321, "width": 159.91712707182322, "height": 22.885416666666586}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Page 5 of 8", "bbox": {"x": 1379.8563533627073, "y": 91.54166661174158, "width": 123.36464088397804, "height": 27.462500000000148}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "discrepancies in findings. Future studies investigating the impact of duration of untreated psychosis on glutamatergic indices will be informative in this regard.", "bbox": {"x": 840.2865017897075, "y": 244.49714239400876, "width": 663.0445778356731, "height": 101.44821026325216}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Another consideration are differences of acquisition techniques across various studies. While we used PRESS, Kegeles and colleagues used MEGA-PRESS to quantify Glx. A technical report comparing these acquisition sequences recently reported that spectral", "bbox": {"x": 845.2762408766406, "y": 347.85833324514766, "width": 657.9447513812155, "height": 160.19791666666657}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "values for Glx acquired with a TE = 80 ms PRESS", "bbox": {"x": 840.7071821002664, "y": 512.6333333233831, "width": 662.513812154696, "height": 27.462499999999903}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "sequence correspond to the off-resonance but not the difference spectra acquired with a MEGA-PRESS sequence in healthy volunteers and medicated FEP subjects**", "bbox": {"x": 840.1121302255127, "y": 540.4200277685349, "width": 663.7449682246256, "height": 132.98073229789745}}, {"kind": "text", "text": ". However, as the authors underscore, it", "bbox": {"x": 959.5027622126851, "y": 645.36874998774, "width": 543.7182320441989, "height": 27.462500000000148}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "remains an open question whether glutamatergic values from PRESS spectra are more or less valid than glutamatergic values from MEGA-PRESS difference spectra.", "bbox": {"x": 840.7071823816349, "y": 677.4083333141182, "width": 662.5138121546962, "height": 96.1187499999999}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "A number of studies relied on shorter TE and spectral fitting to derive the levels of glutamatergic compounds that may explain some of the discrepancies. Last, glutamatergic alterations may vary depending on the region of interest studied, with most studies targeting the frontal regions, specifically the MPFC reporting elevations in glutamatergic compounds, while many other studies targeting more dorsal brain regions, e.g., the dorsal anterior cingulate cortex, found no elevations*.", "bbox": {"x": 840.7071820890737, "y": 778.1041665012376, "width": 662.513812154696, "height": 292.9333333333333}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Contrary to our hypothesis, we observed no decrease in Glx after 6 or 16 weeks of treatment with risperidone. The lack of changes in metabolite levels stands in contrast with a literature review suggesting that antipsychotic medications may affect glutamate levels, with an estimated 6.5% reduction in Glx across brain regions* However, several studies report no antipsychotic induced changest, or regionally selective reductionst in glutamatergic measurements. It is possible that variable antipsychotic medications have diverse effects on the", "bbox": {"x": 840.4064470756067, "y": 1075.361402269262, "width": 662.9382573754748, "height": 330.2935595353444}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "glutamate system. Investigating differential drug effects on brain metabolite levels in an animal model,", "bbox": {"x": 840.7071825429722, "y": 1409.7416665198461, "width": 657.9447513812156, "height": 59.50208333333337}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "McLoughlin and colleagues report antipsychotic induced reductions of glutamate in the frontal cortex only with clozapine and olanzapine, but not risperidone, haloperidol, or aripiprazole*?", "bbox": {"x": 838.9365040160101, "y": 1467.67935520004, "width": 665.4820005300953, "height": 141.92053151528046}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "This is further supported by", "bbox": {"x": 1123.988950044823, "y": 1574.516666648834, "width": 374.6629834254143, "height": 27.462499999999903}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "experiments with the NMDA receptor blocker ketamine, a popular pharmacological model of psychosis 1.1248-53 showing that pretreatment with high dose olanzapine, but not risperidone attenuates ketamine induced alterations in brain metabolismiằ_", "bbox": {"x": 839.9116273291928, "y": 1603.9058279562375, "width": 663.765814006658, "height": 168.73609665632233}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Strengths and limitations", "bbox": {"x": 840.7071820639334, "y": 1807.947916642577, "width": 292.41988950276243, "height": 27.462499999999903}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Our findings have to be interpreted in context of a number of strengths and limitations. We enrolled exclusively medication-naive FEP and a group of HC matched on key demographic characteristics in this prospective longitudinal trial. Even though smoking rates differed between groups, metabolites did not.", "bbox": {"x": 840.7071820588178, "y": 1839.9874999847686, "width": 662.513812154696, "height": 196.81458333333336}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "To minimize", "bbox": {"x": 1334.1657432672193, "y": 2004.7624995422918, "width": 164.48618784530382, "height": 27.462499999999903}}], "ms": 581.901459}